  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nMonitoring 50-60% rain chances late this weekend: \n\nThe ridge forecast to be over the region Thursday and Friday is \nprogged to be quite amplified/meridional. Global ensemble members \nare depicting a low-amplitude shortwave trough to progress through \nthe west side of this ridge Friday and Saturday. This will shove a \nwarm front eastward toward our area and bring 50-60% chances of \nshowers and storms this weekend.\n\nDue to the amplified, but thin and meridional nature of the ridge \npaired with the low amplitude of the wave, model members have been \nstruggling a bit on timing and placement of the shortwave trough. \nHowever, in deviation from the forecast 24 hours ago, models do seem \nto be converging on the scenario of the shortwave taking an east-\nsoutheastward turn as it enters Kansas and Missouri, leading to \nincreased confidence in seeing some sort of precipitation this \nweekend (chances are up from 20-40% 24 hours ago).\n\nWhile members have converged a bit on a solution, the timing \ncontinues to be pushed back towards the late Saturday night into \nSunday morning timeframe. During this time, while the surface warm \nfront will not be quite in our region yet, the elevated warm front \nshould be in the area, with a 25-30 kt nocturnal low-level jet \ndeveloping over the area. This should produce at least scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms overnight Saturday. At the moment, both \ninstability and shear look a tad meager in global model ensemble \nmembers, keeping any appreciable severe risk very low for this round \nof rain chances.\n\n\nShower and thunderstorm potential sometime next week:\n\nFollowing the initial low-amplitude wave, almost all ensemble \nclusters depict a deep and meridional trough swinging through the \nCONUS early next week, which will trigger stronger southerly surface \nflow and warm air advection as the ridge pivots eastward. This will \nbriefly increase highs toward the middle and upper 80s Sunday and \nMonday.\n\nWhile all model members depict a potent trough and front moving \nthrough, there are some differences on timing. Earliest appears to \nbe Monday night (15-40% chance), mean timing is Tuesday into Tuesday \nnight (60-70% chance), and latest is Wednesday night or Thursday \nmorning (15-30% chance). The dynamic nature of the trough and  front \nwill surely produce some showers and thunderstorms, but the severe \nrisk is still up in the air. shear will be plentiful as is per usual \nfor entering fall season, but instability on global models looks a \ntad meager. Trends will continue to be monitored, but a decent \namount of rain looks likely whenever the front moves through as the \nCPC forecasts a 50-60% chance for above normal precipitation for the \nperiod. For some context, though, normal precipitation for the \nperiod is 0.75-1.00 inches. Since the front is expected to move \nthrough rather quickly (chances are spread amongst days due to \ntiming differences in ensembles), this equates to a 50-60% chance \nfor at least 0.75-1.00 inches in some areas with this round of \nprecipitation.",
